Concurrency design for gisp
============================

This document captures design decisions and open questions for the concurrency
primitives in gisp. Implement nothing here until the decisions are settled.


1. Environment model for `go`
------------------------------
Currently `go` shares the caller's *Env chain with the goroutine. Two options:

  A. Shared (current)
     The goroutine reads and writes the same environment as the caller.
     - Natural for patterns where the goroutine mutates a shared variable.
     - Reads are generally safe (env is mostly written at definition time).
     - Concurrent setq on the same symbol is a data race; no locking today.
     - Matches Go's own goroutine model (shared heap, explicit sync).

  B. Isolated (copy-on-spawn)
     A shallow copy of the env frame is made at goroutine spawn time.
     - The goroutine sees a snapshot of the env at the moment of (go ...).
     - Mutations inside the goroutine are invisible to the caller and
       vice-versa, eliminating races without any locking.
     - Already achievable today by wrapping the body in (let ...):
         (go (let (x y z) (do-work x y z)))
     - A copy-on-spawn model would bake this in automatically.

  Open question: keep shared (simpler, more powerful, racy) or isolate
  (safer, slightly surprising for users coming from a Lisp background)?

  Note: if channels are added (see §3), they become the canonical way to
  pass values between goroutines, making full isolation easier to justify.


2. Waiting for completion
--------------------------
Several options, roughly in order of increasing complexity:

  A. Channels as the only sync primitive (Unix philosophy)
     Don't add a special wait mechanism. Instead, the goroutine signals
     completion by sending on a channel the caller created:

         (setq done (chan))
         (go (do-work) (send done 1))
         (recv done)   ; blocks until goroutine finishes

     Simple, composable, no new concepts beyond channels.

  B. `go` returns a future / handle
     (setq f (go (compute)))
     (wait f)            ; blocks until done, returns the last value

     Convenient but requires a new type (Future or Handle). The return
     value threading is slightly tricky with the current Eval model.

  C. WaitGroup as a first-class type
     (setq wg (waitgroup))
     (wg-add wg 1)
     (go (do-work) (wg-done wg))
     (wg-wait wg)

     Mirrors Go's sync.WaitGroup directly. Verbose but familiar to Go
     programmers. Can be built on top of option A internally.

  Recommendation: start with A (channels only), add B later if the
  boilerplate proves painful in practice.


3. Channels
------------
Proposed surface syntax, modeled on Go but Lisp-friendly:

  ; creation
  (chan)          ; unbuffered channel  →  chan[any]
  (chan n)        ; buffered channel of capacity n

  ; send / receive
  (send ch val)   ; send val to ch  (blocks if full / no receiver)
  (recv ch)       ; receive from ch (blocks until a value is available)
                  ; returns the value; on a closed channel returns nil

  ; close
  (close ch)      ; close the channel

  ; select — wait on multiple channel operations (stretch goal)
  (select
    ((recv ch1) (fn val ...))
    ((send ch2 x) (...))
    (default (...)))   ; optional non-blocking default arm

  Implementation notes:
  - Channel type wraps a Go chan any.
  - (recv ch) on a closed, drained channel should return (nil false)
    or just nil — decide based on how multi-value returns are handled.
  - select maps directly to Go's select statement; may require a new
    List variant or special form rather than a builtin.


4. Ordering / phasing
----------------------
Suggested implementation order:

  1. Settle the environment model decision (§1).
  2. Add channels: (chan), (send), (recv), (close).
  3. Validate that channels alone are sufficient as a wait primitive.
  4. Add (select) if needed.
  5. Revisit future/handle (§2B) based on real usage.


5. Race safety
---------------
Even with isolated envs, the gisp interpreter itself is not thread-safe
today (e.g., the global `builtins` map is read-only after init — fine;
but the global symbol table is written at parse time — needs a sync.Mutex
or a RWMutex if goroutines ever parse new code).

Action item: audit global mutable state before shipping concurrent programs
that parse code at runtime.
